 ## Changelog
 
+- 2026-10-24 (3): FIX, sprint started on walk, stop, walk (player: it must be a double tap of forward, not a quick walk-stop-walk). Tested standalone (`sprint.rs` is std-only; rustc 1.75): its 8 tests pass; the rest of mc-rs not compiled, not run, not checked on a device.
+    - Cause (`Sprint::tick`): Retro Sprint arms its 7 tick window on ANY first press of forward (`sprintStartTicks == 0` -> 7), however long it is held, so a few steps, a stop and a new press inside the window sprinted. The mod's keyboard has no such case worth a name; a d-pad walk does.
+    - Fix: a press only counts as the first tap if it is released within `sprint::TAP` ticks (4 = 0.2 s; a finger tap or a click is 2..3). Releasing a tap arms `WINDOW` (7 ticks, now counted from the release, not the first press, so tap + gap are separate); releasing a longer press (a walk) disarms it. The second press of a window sprints as before; the key / L3 still sprints at once; every other rule (ground, `can`, wall, water, 30 s) is unchanged. New field `Sprint.held`. `lib.rs` is untouched.
+    - Test `double_tap_starts_a_sprint_and_a_hold_or_a_walk_does_not` (replaces `double_tap_starts_a_sprint_and_a_hold_does_not`): tap-release-tap, a tap of exactly `TAP` ticks with a 3 tick gap, a hold of 100, walk (`TAP + 1`) stop walk (no sprint, and a real double tap right after still works), a late second press.
+    - ponytail: `TAP` and `WINDOW` are the two knobs (raise `TAP` to 6 if a slow tap on the d-pad does not register, lower it to 3 if a short step still sprints); a stick flicked up, back and up again counts the same way. UNVERIFIED on a device: the feel of both numbers.
 - 2026-10-24 (2): stamina on the sprint, a port of Stamina (insane96mcp, NeoForge 26.1.2; `Stamina-26_1_2.zip`, read: `StaminaFeature` (tick, jump, slowdown), `StaminaHandler`, `StaminaFeatureClient` (overlay, debug entry), `IStaminaModifier`, the changelog, `en_us.json`, the two overlay textures; the enchantments, the Vigour effect, runes, data-pack conditions, command, network sync and Mobs Properties Randomness hooks have no counterpart here) (written WITHOUT a full toolchain: `world/stamina.rs` and `vitals.rs` were type-checked and their tests run in a Linux scratch crate (6 pass); the whole crate does not build on a host (`ndk-sys` is Android only), so the `lib.rs` and `hud.rs` edits are NOT compiled; +5 tests `stamina::tests::*`, +2 asserts in `vitals::tests::damage_like_java`, the `STAMINA` sprite in `hud::tests::gui_sheet_has_the_sprites`; run `cargo test --lib --release -- stamina vitals hud` first).
     - `world/stamina.rs` (new, pure): `Stamina::tick` is `onPlayerTick` once per 20 Hz tick, with the mod's defaults. Full = 200 (10 per health point), x(1 - 0.025 per armor point), x health / 20 as the health falls. A sprint costs 1 a tick (x0.8 when not hit or hurt in the last 300 ticks), a jump 10; the bar at 0 locks (`consumeStamina`, jump included). Not sprinting it regenerates 2 a tick (x0.6 locked, x(1 - 0.025 armor)); the lock opens when the stamina of the tick before is 40% of the maximum; at 20% health or less the bar is emptied and locked and does not regenerate. `speed`: x0.9 while locked, x0.85 more while sprinting on under 40 or under 20% (`ADD_MULTIPLIED_TOTAL`, they multiply). `halves(health)` = the hearts overlay's `ceil(stamina / (max / health))`.
     - `Vitals.stamina`: health and armor are its maximum, `Vitals::hurt` marks combat (the mod's `LivingDamageEvent.Post` for any damage, falls and lava too), and every `Vitals::default()` (respawn, new world) fills it, as the mod's respawn does.
